# Dreamscape Browser Agent Access

Dreamscape runs at `http://127.0.0.1:9220`.

## Required local configuration

- Set `"enable_dev": true` in `config.json`, then restart the server.
- Set `devLoginKey` in `secure.config.json`.
- Never commit, quote, log, or paste the key into chat or rule files.

The agent login route requires all three conditions on every request:

- the direct TCP peer (`req.socket.remoteAddress`) is loopback;
- `enable_dev` is true; and
- the request supplies the valid `devLoginKey`.

Forwarded headers such as `X-Forwarded-For` and `X-Real-IP` are never trusted for
this gate. A remote request receives 403 even if it has a stolen development key
or spoofs a loopback forwarded address. An existing admin or `dev_admin` session
does not bypass the key requirement.

SSH local forwarding remains supported. For example, forwarding a local port to
server-side `127.0.0.1:9220` makes the connection's direct peer loopback on the
Dreamscape host; the key and `enable_dev` checks still apply.

## Preferred login flow

1. Read `devLoginKey` locally from `secure.config.json` without printing it.
2. Set the browser request header `Authorization: Bearer <devLoginKey>`.
3. Navigate to `http://127.0.0.1:9220/agent`.
4. The server creates a `dev_admin` session and returns a no-store bootstrap
   page. That page unregisters existing workers, fetches
   `GET /agent/assets.json` with the new session cookie, preloads the app-shell
   CSS/JS URLs into the browser HTTP cache, then opens
   `http://127.0.0.1:9220/app?agent=1`.
5. Clear the temporary Authorization header after the redirect. The session
   cookie handles subsequent HTTP, WebSocket, and `/agent/assets.*` requests.

For browser tooling that cannot set request headers, the loopback-only fallback
is `http://127.0.0.1:9220/agent?auth=<URL-encoded-devLoginKey>`. Do not show or
retain the completed URL in chat, screenshots, logs, or committed files.

Run `/agent` at least once in each browser context, even if it already has a
session, so an older worker cannot serve a cached app shell. After that cleanup,
open `http://127.0.0.1:9220/app?agent=1` directly and keep `agent=1` on
app-shell navigations.

## Agent mode behavior

`agent=1` is the cacheless browser-agent mode:

- the `/agent` bootstrap removes existing Dreamscape worker registrations;
- after navigation is no longer worker-controlled, Cache Storage entries are deleted;
- a page already controlled by an old worker reloads once after cleanup;
- no new service worker is registered;
- startup skips service-worker manifest download and install waits;
- later service-worker cache update requests are ignored for that page.
- desktop mode is forced at boot and `galleryWindowMode` is set to `windowed`,
  regardless of the browser agent viewport width.

Do not wait for a service worker, install wizard, or offline-cache download in
agent sessions. `/agent` preloads app-shell CSS and JS into a 120-second
private HTTP cache (`dev_admin` sessions only) so `/app?agent=1` does not
refetch those files one-by-one. Optional `GET /agent/assets.zip` returns the
same asset set as a single download for tools; the bootstrap does not unzip it
because Cache Storage cannot satisfy `<script src>` without a worker.

The successful `/agent` flow lands directly in the authenticated desktop shell;
do not automate the PIN pad or open `/` first.

`GET /agent` still requires the development key on every request. `GET
/agent/assets.json` and `GET /agent/assets.zip` accept that key **or** the
`dev_admin` session created by `/agent`, and remain loopback-only.

## Warn connected users before a restart

`POST /agent/broadcast` uses the same loopback + `enable_dev` + development-key
gate as `GET /agent`. It pushes `agent_notice` to every WebSocket client as a
toast or confirmation dialog. Call this before restarting the server or doing
other work that would interrupt an active user.

```bash
# Read the key locally; do not print it.
# display: toast (default) or dialog. level: info | warning | error | success.
curl -sS -X POST 'http://127.0.0.1:9220/agent/broadcast' \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer ${DEV_LOGIN_KEY}" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"message":"The server will restart in a few seconds.","display":"dialog","level":"warning"}'
```

JSON body: `message` (required, plain text), optional `title`, `display` /
`mode` (`toast` or `dialog`), `level` / `type`, `timeout` (ms or `false`).
Success returns `{ success, id, display, level, title, timeout, clients }`.
Wait a moment after a dialog broadcast so users can see it before you restart.

## Troubleshooting

- `403` from `/agent`: direct peer is not loopback, or the supplied key is invalid.
- `404` from `/agent`: `enable_dev` is false.
- `500` from `/agent`: `devLoginKey` is absent from `secure.config.json`.
- `401` or `403`: the Authorization header/query key is missing or invalid.
- `401` from `/agent/assets.json` during bootstrap: the `dev_admin` session
  cookie was not stored; re-run `/agent` with the key rather than opening
  `/app` first.
- Login page appears: authenticate through `/agent`; do not automate the PIN pad.
